WEBVTT

00:00.640 --> 00:04.720
Hello everyone, welcome back to another episode of the Grand Prixquel Podcast where we discuss

00:04.720 --> 00:09.280
all things feeder series. On today's episode we are going to be doing a recap of the F2 and

00:09.280 --> 00:15.120
F3 weekends at the Red Bull Ring, as well as doing a preview of the upcoming E4 Championship.

00:15.120 --> 00:18.800
Our hosts for today are myself, Robin, Alex, Nova and Peter.

00:18.800 --> 00:29.920
Yeah, so starting with Formula 2 looking to qualifying. I mean, I think we should look

00:29.920 --> 00:35.680
towards who is going to be on the sprint front row. Here we had Montoya in 10th and Bennett in

00:35.680 --> 00:40.800
P9, making them our front row for the sprint race. I'm sorry, what?

00:41.760 --> 00:48.160
But I just want to point out in general that I think this weekend kind of, I think Red Bull

00:48.160 --> 00:53.280
Ring is a great place and one of those tracks where you can really see the strengths of teams

00:53.840 --> 01:00.560
and I think it's really interesting to see that such a, more typical track seems to be

01:00.560 --> 01:05.760
fitting for Bennett. I mean, the whole quali session was a bit weird with

01:06.720 --> 01:12.720
Noel Leon already doing his lap fairly early on and then just being fine with it.

01:14.080 --> 01:21.280
Kind of, you know, boss move, if you ask me. I feel like it tells a lot about strengths of

01:21.280 --> 01:26.560
different drivers and different teams because y'all have to correct me, but I swear that

01:26.560 --> 01:33.920
Varrone and Herta were down pretty low, and for both of them it's a track that they have not

01:33.920 --> 01:39.280
driven or not driven in quite a long time, and I think it's really telling how

01:41.040 --> 01:48.000
you can see the strengths of drivers coming with this experience and on the other end also

01:48.000 --> 01:59.280
the teams that I'm a bit surprised about because I did expect Invicta to be the clear strongest

01:59.280 --> 02:08.080
again, because I swear that in the last years tracks, like this was really fast paced and you need to

02:09.120 --> 02:17.120
be really quick and also the strategy to get them on track in the right moment, has always been

02:17.120 --> 02:24.880
their strong suit. I mean, last year Leonardo Fornaroli had his pole position here because

02:25.760 --> 02:33.200
back then, I swear we’ve all said, this track can really fit the Invicta crew like the way they operated.

02:34.160 --> 02:39.760
Yeah, I think it's also- I feel like there's not really a clear top team in terms of pace like

02:39.760 --> 02:44.960
there maybe was last year. Like last time out Invicta were really strong at Spain,

02:44.960 --> 02:50.720
especially with how good they are on the tyres whereas over here it wasn't so strong, but I mean

02:50.720 --> 02:55.360
Camara is still consistently getting those points, which is the main thing in F2 is consistency.

02:56.240 --> 03:00.720
So guess we’ll maybe have to wait and see because we still have a fair few number of rounds to go.

03:01.760 --> 03:07.760
Yeah, but I feel like it's very clear that, the German commentary also mentioned that,

03:07.760 --> 03:14.880
Campos in quite a lot of series made clear steps forward during the winter break and they seem

03:15.600 --> 03:22.960
to be really really up there and really fighting for being the quickest

03:22.960 --> 03:29.040
car, you know? In every series but Eurocup-3, they've somehow regressed in the one series

03:29.040 --> 03:34.640
I care about. I think that’s just the Alex Powell curse and of course you’re here to bring him up

03:34.640 --> 03:42.400
in an episode where he's not even meant to be mentioned. Whatever. I have a feeling this is not the first

03:42.400 --> 03:50.080
Campos pole but apparently it is. I did not think that. I feel like both the drivers have been

03:50.080 --> 03:57.280
so strong and so quick. I think like Tsolov is always somewhere in the top five so he's-

03:57.280 --> 04:02.480
or top 10. He's always starting somewhere around there. Was he not on pole in Monaco?

04:02.480 --> 04:07.600
No, Camara took pole in Monaco, I'm pretty sure. Oh yeah, and then oh- I remember then he like

04:07.600 --> 04:13.360
fluffed it he went off into the-. Camara took it at the very end of qualifying.

04:13.360 --> 04:18.640
Yeah of course sorry, my mistake. Anyways, I think moving on to the sprint race. I mean I

04:18.640 --> 04:23.200
think the main thing, I'm just still surprised at how well Bennett has been doing I think-

04:23.200 --> 04:29.120
He played a really smart race. He got the initial lead then, yeah he lost the lead but he still

04:29.120 --> 04:35.200
kept pace with Montoya and kind of, he attacked. Last lap moves as always- I mean kind of aura farming

04:35.200 --> 04:41.200
with that but it's still pretty cool. And yeah I mean, hopefully we begin to see more of Bennett

04:41.200 --> 04:46.640
you know, because having a win, albeit in a sprint, is still going to be a massive boost of confidence

04:46.640 --> 04:54.720
for any driver. I mean interestingly enough, Trident has been the better team than Prema

04:54.720 --> 05:02.720
so far this season and I think Bennett is a driver that should be able to do it. His career has just

05:02.720 --> 05:09.680
been so incredibly weird. Yeah, and towards the end it was also interesting how quickly Montoya

05:09.680 --> 05:14.960
fell away cause Villagomez was suddenly all over the back of him as well. Like one more lap

05:14.960 --> 05:19.680
and he'd have been overtaken by Villagomez, I reckon, because he's literally all over the back

05:19.680 --> 05:25.600
of him throughout that final third sector where, you can't really overtake in that third sector

05:25.680 --> 05:33.040
that's why I think Villagomez didn't get past but. I mean I assume for a lot of drivers especially

05:33.040 --> 05:40.640
when in the top, we saw the Red Bull Ring is not the easiest on the tyres. I think I could imagine

05:40.640 --> 05:48.320
if you played the tyre game the right way and if you also took the DRS which is fairly good around

05:48.320 --> 05:55.840
here I think that's a really good way to be up there, and I feel like we saw that with drivers

05:55.840 --> 06:01.840
like Villagomez really work out. It felt almost like an F3 race in the way they were all having to

06:01.840 --> 06:07.120
keep their tyres alive and that came really important towards the end. But I think generally

06:07.120 --> 06:15.840
it was a fairly comparably uneventful race like…oh no, it was not like- the first lap was a bit

06:15.840 --> 06:22.080
chaotic you know, when there was a lot of incidents. I felt like it was weird because it was a lot of

06:22.080 --> 06:27.840
smaller incidents but it all bundled up into one bigger incident. I mean we never really got a

06:27.840 --> 06:33.840
proper replay of that cause the camera was really far away, you couldn't see s***. Now the

06:33.840 --> 06:39.200
problem was rather for me that there was so much happening simultaneously that it's really hard

06:39.200 --> 06:45.440
to also judge who's at fault for what, because you have in the first lap obviously a lot of cars

06:45.840 --> 06:52.720
going side by side through the corners, and at some point I think it was Camara who hit someone

06:52.720 --> 06:59.600
in the back, I don't remember who the person spun around, took Tsolov with them, for some reason one of

06:59.600 --> 07:09.280
the AIX cars was also involved, had to almost stop, there's just a lot of drama and I think

07:09.280 --> 07:15.440
that's kind of really typical for F2 but it's generally the only thing from that weekend

07:15.440 --> 07:22.240
not from that weekend, from that race, that I remember as being really typically F2 fashion, the rest of it

07:22.240 --> 07:29.920
was pretty, you know, tame for F2. Yeah, also speaking of Tsolov, he made a good recovery

07:29.920 --> 07:34.720
of that incident because it was impressive I mean he- I've said this quite a few times on the

07:34.720 --> 07:41.120
podcast now, but he continues to impress me with what he's doing, cause yes the Red Bull Ring is

07:41.120 --> 07:46.160
relatively easier to overtake on compared to other tracks, especially

07:46.160 --> 07:51.440
with those DRS zones at the start, but he's also just making those moves when he needs to and he's

07:51.440 --> 07:58.560
kept the cars alive. And yeah, it was a shame also to see Colton Herta to get Mechachromed, because

07:58.560 --> 08:02.720
he was when we were on the top 10 when it happened and I was hoping, maybe he's keeping his

08:02.720 --> 08:07.520
tires alive, maybe we can see a few more points from him, but the engine just kind of gave up on

08:07.520 --> 08:16.480
him. I'm really disappointed in Hitech this season because at the very least in 2024, they were

08:16.480 --> 08:24.640
one of those really strong teams and I expected a lot more from there. Even last year they like- Luke

08:24.640 --> 08:30.800
won last year I think with them. I don’t remember, I just remember when Paul Aron was with the team and

08:30.960 --> 08:37.920
we said a lot because we, we all said that, who was it? Amaury Cordeel, I think, was really underperforming

08:37.920 --> 08:44.080
when the car would have been a lot better and I feel like it's the entire way around now where we

08:44.080 --> 08:49.280
have two really great drivers who could perform, and I think are performing to the best of what they

08:49.280 --> 08:55.360
can currently do with their experience and the things that they do, but there's just so many

08:55.600 --> 09:00.480
issues, so many small issues or so many things that just don't go

09:00.480 --> 09:06.720
right, and it could be the fact that Hitech has had some weird stuff going on in the background

09:06.720 --> 09:13.200
but like. I'm a bit disappointed. I think beyond that, I don't have too much more to say about the

09:13.200 --> 09:22.400
sprint race. I think it was a solid race just typically F2, bit of chaos I mean

09:22.400 --> 09:30.560
we had quite the surprising podium I feel like because you don't always see a podium

09:31.520 --> 09:38.480
including like drivers who are doing their first podiums of their whole F2 career, or if the whole

09:38.480 --> 09:47.120
season. Pretty sure all of them had their first podium for the season here. Wait, no,

09:47.200 --> 09:51.520
Villagomez at least has had podiums before in F2, but for the season

09:53.040 --> 09:59.520
I think it's even his first points. And somehow even after that sprint podium he’s still last

09:59.520 --> 10:08.480
in the standings. Oh. What do we got now? Uh, the feature race. Yeah I mean, uh Tsolov again going

10:08.480 --> 10:15.600
for the win but. I mean the battle between Tsolov, Dunne and Mini and I mean again, Tsolov and Dunne

10:15.600 --> 10:20.880
racing each other cleanly. Have they changed? They did this last week, they did last time out

10:20.880 --> 10:27.600
in Spain, they’re doing it again. They're both reformed people, almost it’s as if. I'm really glad because I

10:27.600 --> 10:34.400
think we're lining up for an incredible title fight and what we saw in that feature, I think

10:34.400 --> 10:41.120
was a great race, because we were really lucky to have quite a lot of drivers fighting for the lead,

10:41.120 --> 10:46.400
drivers whose lead also means a lot for the championship and the things and the points

10:46.400 --> 10:53.840
that they're scoring. Like, we have once again Gabriele Mini, who finished P2 and continues his very, very

10:53.840 --> 11:02.560
solid streak of podiums, and we talked about it earlier, I don't remember what, but it's really

11:02.560 --> 11:10.160
important in F2 to consistently score points and so far, he has scored points in every single race

11:10.160 --> 11:18.240
but one and he's had, I think seven podiums in 12 races like that's a really, really good

11:18.880 --> 11:26.160
statistic. And I think a lot of drivers will say they want to win obviously, but there is reason why

11:26.160 --> 11:32.640
he's currently above Tsolov in the standings, even though Tsolov has three more wins than him, because

11:32.640 --> 11:39.200
you know Tsolov either has like win or there is uh oftentimes also more retirements, also more

11:39.200 --> 11:44.960
incidents that send him to the back of the pack so. I think we're lining up to see a really

11:44.960 --> 11:51.920
interesting battle up there, and I think as you’ve mentioned it's great to see the way that they

11:51.920 --> 11:57.280
drove all cleanly, and even though they were fighting quite a lot also with just the whole

11:58.240 --> 12:04.640
group of Mini, Tsolov, Camara and Dunne, no one of them DNF'd, I think there was a really strong

12:04.640 --> 12:11.760
result and really strong showing for them. So I mean, Dunne's end of the race was interesting to say

12:11.760 --> 12:18.080
the least. I mean not to- maybe he had some clutch issues or something but. I assumed he just cooked

12:18.080 --> 12:24.560
his tires because like there was a lot of, a lot of fights, and I could really imagine that stuff

12:24.560 --> 12:30.560
really going on your tires after a while. I mean, he had clutch issues at the start I believe and-

12:31.040 --> 12:36.400
but also he did run wide at the start of the finish straight going off track so that maybe

12:36.400 --> 12:43.520
caused some minor damage as well. Like I said, I mean, look at the championship it is, I mean, Tsolov and

12:43.520 --> 12:50.160
Mini are only separated by two points. Incredibly close up there. And even the likes of Camara and

12:50.160 --> 12:58.880
Dunne aren't too further back, with Camara being 24 points behind Tsolov and Dunne only two points

12:58.960 --> 13:06.320
behind Tsolov- I mean behind Camara. I mean we still have quite a few races, I think we're at

13:07.120 --> 13:16.720
almost half the season and I think what we've seen also you can- I mean look at Tsolov’s stats you can

13:16.720 --> 13:22.320
win two races and then do not score points in the next four races. He’s actually won three feature

13:22.320 --> 13:30.000
races. Yeah but I was looking at the Australia feature and Miami sprint which won and then

13:30.000 --> 13:36.640
did not score points for four races and then he won again. The fact he’s won three feature races

13:36.640 --> 13:43.120
that's 75- pretty much 75 percent of his points have come from that. But also I think it's really interesting

13:43.120 --> 13:49.920
then to think about what Campos is doing, what Campos is clearly doing right that they seem to

13:49.920 --> 13:56.480
always put them on the correct strategy for the win and that they clearly have the pace to do it.

13:56.775 --> 14:00.094
Even Leon is there in P5 in the championship.

14:00.240 --> 14:08.240
I feel like he's one of those drivers where we don't really– I mean I think he's a solid driver

14:08.240 --> 14:14.560
but I think if we compare him to other drivers who have relationships to F1 teams who are below

14:14.560 --> 14:21.600
him like Dino Beganovic or Kush Maini or even Colton Herta, I think no one really expected him

14:21.600 --> 14:27.680
to be that up far but considering the team and car, he really should be. I mean, Campos have

14:27.680 --> 14:32.480
become underrated, like last year they also performed well. I mean, 2024 with Hadjar, they were good in the title

14:32.480 --> 14:38.000
fight; last year, they had Arvid Lindblad. They’ve always been kinda up there, but as you said they've

14:38.000 --> 14:43.920
taken a step further almost. I feel like what they're doing right now, I have not seen them

14:43.920 --> 14:50.400
do a mistake. Like, I feel like in 2024 we complained quite a lot about, especially

14:50.400 --> 14:56.400
strategy mistakes that they've made, and just like little things that didn't line up. Last year,

14:56.400 --> 15:03.520
it was the same and I feel like this year, it just really kind of all falls together and we seem to

15:03.520 --> 15:12.480
have done the best. But I think even though it was a good race, I think it was besides the battle

15:12.480 --> 15:20.000
between the leaders of the championship, it was actually surprisingly little going on, except for

15:20.000 --> 15:27.920
the fact when Boya suddenly went up into flames. That was a real Formula 2 moment. That's always got to

15:27.920 --> 15:33.200
happen at some point we've got to have someone be Mecachromed in every race so. It was really impressive

15:33.200 --> 15:39.040
that Herta didn't like just crash straight into the back of him. I could not be a racing driver

15:39.040 --> 15:46.320
because that would stress me out. I would just panic and crash. What if my car turned fire next?

15:47.440 --> 15:53.840
Mini in the cool down room after he like, went past Boya, he was so funny and I feel like

15:53.840 --> 16:02.720
he's spiritually what I am on the podcast. Just dramatic yapping constantly. It was, it was great.

16:02.720 --> 16:08.480
Overall I'm really grateful that the way it seems right now, there's really no

16:09.120 --> 16:15.920
favorite for the championship title. Like last year, we knew that the Invicta was the strongest

16:15.920 --> 16:22.480
car and so we all hoped others could also be in a title fight, but for me it was pretty clear that

16:22.480 --> 16:30.080
it was not likely to happen, but at the moment, I'm going to be honest, I don't think MP is the

16:30.080 --> 16:34.960
strongest car; and I feel like they've made quite a few mistakes, especially with Ollie Goethe which I

16:34.960 --> 16:43.440
know a lot of others we know are kind of mad about, and Gabriele Mini, he's doing a good season

16:43.440 --> 16:48.720
but how do you do that against two drivers who are considered to be in better cars with

16:48.720 --> 16:57.200
Tsolov and Camara right behind him. And, Alex Dunne is, I think the one who was, from the bunch of them,

16:57.200 --> 17:02.560
the highest in the championship last season, so it's a really interesting class and it's lining

17:02.560 --> 17:08.080
up to be a lot more interesting than I thought it would be, which is great. Yeah, and of course

17:08.080 --> 17:13.840
as we've alluded to in the team championship. surprise, surprise! Campos is first. Who would have

17:13.840 --> 17:21.680
expected that? Unless anyone has anything more to say about F2, I think we’ll move on to F3 now.

17:21.840 --> 17:28.640
We can move on to F3. Before we get into any of the racing, we have a new driver for the– we had a

17:28.640 --> 17:34.400
new driver for the AIX seat: Salim Hanna was in; and I thought it was really funny because I was expecting

17:34.400 --> 17:39.760
KNB, like if they were to pick any of the three FREC drivers, I thought it was going to be KNB,

17:40.480 --> 17:45.680
but it wasn't. And especially it’d have been funny to have him after his last time out at the Red Bull

17:45.680 --> 17:53.760
Ring, that was quite eventful– sorry, Nova but, that was quite, it was quite eventful to say the least.

17:54.960 --> 18:02.480
It was, but Salim was a surprise, honestly. I think out of all the drivers that were put forward as a

18:03.440 --> 18:10.800
possible like, filler I wasn't expecting Salim at all. It's just I guess whoever can pay. Yeah.

18:11.440 --> 18:16.960
And luckily for us, we are recording on a Wednesday, which means we already know who is in

18:16.960 --> 18:21.440
for Silverstone. We don't need to wait and say ‘Oh my god, have they announced it after the episode?’

18:22.320 --> 18:28.160
We know. Um, they're bringing Escotto back. Who knows, they've just announced actually someone else.

18:28.720 --> 18:35.440
I guess there's so many other series happening this week with, of course, there's E4 happening,

18:35.440 --> 18:43.760
there's FREC going on, EC3 is racing, I think GB3 is as well. The thing is, IndyNXT is on too.

18:43.760 --> 18:48.880
But I guess, is there any series which was not racing this week where a driver could realistically

18:48.880 --> 18:57.280
step up to F3? Super Formula Lights. Evan Giltaire. Bring Evan Giltaire into F3. I really would have

18:57.280 --> 19:04.160
liked to see either Giltaire or Bohra show up but um, it's Escotto again. Don't know why he's

19:04.160 --> 19:12.160
prioritizing an F3 cameo over his IndyNXT season but whatever. And Salim, he's actually

19:12.160 --> 19:19.440
done a solid job in qualifying because he's the highest AIX so I mean, good job. I mean the other two AIX drivers

19:19.440 --> 19:27.280
are… yeah, but still a good achievement to have, I think. That sounds so mean, oh my god. I feel

19:27.280 --> 19:35.200
like- Wait, I need to, I need to look up who the AIX drivers actually are. It's Yevan David and

19:35.200 --> 19:40.960
Fernando Barichello. That's both drivers who do not have a lot of experience, especially like

19:40.960 --> 19:49.120
it's their first season in F3 and, I mean, I'm pretty sure Brad Benavides also would

19:49.120 --> 19:56.400
outqualify them pretty clearly. Yeah, and also I guess Salim, he's been to the track before which helps.

19:56.800 --> 20:04.640
He’s of course, was here for FREC. Yeah, but at the same time, it's, he's a rookie in FREC and last year

20:04.640 --> 20:10.960
was his F4 rookie season as well. So he's- yeah, he's still done quite a good job I think, it's not like

20:10.960 --> 20:17.600
we don't want to take away from that. Considering he's in an AIX, I was a bit impressed, yeah. And

20:17.600 --> 20:24.720
I mean it's quite surprising because Nael, where was Nael? He's been like, he's been so good in

20:24.800 --> 20:31.200
qualifying and then he's been kind of ghosted, to say the least. Nael and Badoer were both

20:31.200 --> 20:37.680
nowhere. He was only P25 in qualifying, Nael and that was a real surprise cause. Yeah I watched

20:37.680 --> 20:43.520
qualifying, but what happened? Because I cannot imagine that he just did not have the speed.

20:44.720 --> 20:50.640
I think it was probably track limits or something. Like just his laps kept getting deleted and so he

20:50.640 --> 20:56.640
just didn't have any good ones. That's a good explanation and I'm going to accept that as being

20:56.640 --> 21:01.760
the right one. Yeah, especially you know we've seen how strong Campos have been this year.

21:02.640 --> 21:10.080
I mean we had Ugochukwu who was second, Rivera was also in the top 12. Maybe not as strong as they

21:10.080 --> 21:15.200
were in Spain but they're still quite strong here. That's because Spain is their home track and they

21:15.200 --> 21:23.600
know it the best. They will always be kind of the one to beat there because again it is their

21:23.600 --> 21:29.280
home track. It's a track that they have a lot more when they race in, what Robin will know more

21:29.280 --> 21:38.560
about, the Spanish serieses. Again, Alex Powell mention number two. I'm still annoyed that Campos has not

21:38.560 --> 21:43.280
been dominating Eurocup-3, but that's a problem for another episode. I'm not going to-

21:45.600 --> 21:50.880
And then within the sprint race. Rivera, I mean, the way he's come back from injury, has been

21:50.880 --> 21:56.560
really impressive. Of course, he missed the first round because of a back injury. He's come back and he's

21:56.560 --> 22:06.560
been really good and getting a win. He did really good, nice overtake on Wharton. I mean, he's P11

22:06.560 --> 22:14.640
in the standings, even after missing a whole round; so I think that on one hand, that shows the way

22:14.640 --> 22:21.440
that he's been really good at getting used to the cars with arguably, you know, a bit less time

22:21.440 --> 22:27.120
than the others but also obviously he has a good car. He's in a good team. Also I want to talk about

22:27.120 --> 22:32.640
what happened at the start. Maybe I don't want to talk about it. Stromsted took Slater out. Well

22:32.640 --> 22:38.800
technically, it was a racing incident. Okay but, if you watch it, Stromsted just basically oversteered

22:38.800 --> 22:45.360
into Freddie. Freddie gave him room. Kind of what should he have done? Because I mean, I'm pretty

22:45.360 --> 22:51.040
sure that it was also a racing incident because it was the first lap. I just want to blame Stromsted.

22:51.680 --> 22:55.120
I'm just going to blame him because he crashed into Freddie and took Freddie out of the race.

22:55.120 --> 23:00.160
Of course. Yeah, you're biased and I think that's not cool. I think it's really important to keep

23:00.160 --> 23:06.400
in mind that it was the first lap. There was a lot of stuff going on and when you have to decide

23:06.400 --> 23:13.840
between you know, a lot of times they're saying “Hey, I can do this.” and only then, they oversteer

23:13.840 --> 23:20.400
and they look then they realize “Oh sh*t, I can't do this. I actually, you know, overestimated myself.”

23:20.400 --> 23:26.800
At that point, there's no way to take it back. If you go into any situation thinking “how could this

23:26.800 --> 23:33.920
end badly” you'll make a really, really f*cking sh*t racing driver; because if that didn't happen,

23:33.920 --> 23:40.320
he would have had a pretty good start, and if we go from in a hundred situations where it's the

23:40.320 --> 23:46.720
same start, he would not have oversteered into– I don't care, he crashed into Freddie, that's all I.

23:47.280 --> 23:53.600
Maybe Freddie should have just qualified on pole and not been in the way. Stop being biased

23:53.600 --> 24:00.640
Peter. Why can't I be biased? Why can I not be biased? Because I'm against biases. Cause I’m evil.

24:00.640 --> 24:07.920
Moving on, moving on. Clerot had some really good overtakes during the race. There was

24:08.800 --> 24:15.360
one point where he overtook two cars into one corner. I don't remember whether that was the-

24:15.360 --> 24:21.920
but it was really good. He's got a strong weekend overall and yeah. Wharton at the end–

24:22.080 --> 24:30.720
his best one so far. Wharton at the end just fell off completely. He was there in P2 and he just lost

24:30.720 --> 24:39.360
like- he just got swamped by. I could imagine that it is the tyres, yeah, potential tyre overheating,

24:39.360 --> 24:44.800
you know, overdoing the tyres, and suddenly the guys behind you and they've been using the DRS

24:44.800 --> 24:52.000
the whole time, they've been there, and they've not pushed as hard and suddenly you know, they're

24:52.000 --> 24:58.960
dangerous. It was nice to see Nakamura on the podium, I liked that. Was that not the race where

24:58.960 --> 25:06.960
we had like- no, Kanato Le was disqualified but if he was not disqualified, we would have had all four

25:07.600 --> 25:13.440
Japanese drivers in the top 10 which I also found to be quite interesting. There's something about

25:13.440 --> 25:21.120
Austria that the Japanese drivers really like, somehow. Or hate. KNB, there's something, he

25:21.120 --> 25:28.000
doesn't like Austria. I mean, he's part Japanese. He's not Japanese enough. Maybe you need to be

25:28.000 --> 25:34.800
fully Japanese. He hated it so much we made up a new verb for the Austria weekend

25:34.800 --> 25:41.520
actually, I think. But yeah, so good performance from all the Japanese drivers this weekend.

25:41.520 --> 25:49.120
There can only be one Nakamura in Austria and it's not Kean Nakamura-Berta. Oh my god, imagine

25:49.120 --> 25:55.920
the confusion with the commentators if Jin and Kean both raced together in the same weekend.

25:56.480 --> 26:02.720
Nakamura against Nakamura. I mean they did call Jin they called Jin Nakajima at some point in one

26:02.720 --> 26:08.480
of the races so there's only one Nakamura and they're still messing up his name. You know what I just

26:08.480 --> 26:16.160
saw, what I think is actually really hilarious? Brad Benavides only did two rounds out of the

26:16.160 --> 26:24.880
four we've done so far and he's still P18 in the championship and there's so many guys below him.

26:26.480 --> 26:34.000
That's so bad actually. He also only scored points, like, once. That's horrific. I think, someday we

26:34.000 --> 26:40.320
need to have a conversation of if F3 the way it is right now actually makes sense because this is

26:40.320 --> 26:47.600
just sad. Last thing I want to mention about the sprint race is that Enzo was having some kind of

26:47.600 --> 26:56.960
mechanical issue at the start which is why he had like a horrific start; and then I don't know what

26:56.960 --> 27:02.400
McLaughlin was doing but he went off track came back on track and just went straight into the

27:02.400 --> 27:09.040
side of Enzo and his like- they were both fine I think. Wait, I don't remember what happened to

27:09.040 --> 27:14.480
Fionn after that but Enzo was fine at least. And that’s all that matters. We forgot to mention one thing, I

27:14.480 --> 27:21.680
think, and that is the winner of the race. Oh my god and I forgot his name. Ernesto Rivera?

27:21.680 --> 27:27.360
Ernesto Rivera. He's a Red Bull junior at the Red Bull Ring. Tsolov is also a Red Bull junior, we also

27:27.360 --> 27:34.480
didn't mention that. I think I know, they also want– they all want to win everywhere but I think

27:35.360 --> 27:40.800
You’re Ernesto Rivera, and you win your first race at the Red Bull Ring, I think that's special.

27:40.800 --> 27:47.440
Yeah that's really cool. And then again if you're Fionn and you… kind of crash people at the Red Bull

27:47.440 --> 27:53.760
Ring. I mean he's just been struggling the whole season though nothing… this step up has just harmed

27:53.760 --> 28:00.960
him a lot, going straight from British F4 to like– it’s very Tsolov-like what he's done because

28:00.960 --> 28:06.640
of course, Tsolov went from Spanish F4 straight to F3, and he really struggled at the start. But I also

28:06.640 --> 28:14.560
feel like Hitech is just not the best because first of all, Jin Nakamura is kind of- he's doing

28:14.560 --> 28:21.360
good all things considered, but other than that Hitech is just not scoring points and it could

28:21.360 --> 28:30.800
be the fact that their drivers, you know, just aren't ready for it. But I just also think–

28:31.920 --> 28:39.200
I'm going to be honest– if he was in like Campos or Trident, he would definitely be doing better.

28:39.200 --> 28:43.120
But we can't because, he has– of course, done a quite a big step up as well.

28:43.120 --> 28:49.920
I mean he's even in, even Oceania, he did struggle quite a bit. I wouldn't say that he

28:49.920 --> 28:56.480
would automatically be winning and stuff but I think that if he was in- maybe he'd get some

28:56.480 --> 29:04.240
points at least. And another thing that I think is these are circuits that he just does not know

29:04.240 --> 29:13.360
at all and like especially Monaco and Australia; they were two circuits he just had no experience

29:13.360 --> 29:18.960
on. I mean at least, Silverstone, Silverstone would be the only one– Silverstone would be the only one

29:18.960 --> 29:25.680
he knows on the whole calendar. Like I think we make a lot of fun of like drivers who you know

29:26.320 --> 29:31.200
if a driver from Italian F4 would make the step, we would make fun but in the last – I know this

29:31.200 --> 29:37.440
season we only have Italian circuits– but in the last few seasons, we've also oftentimes had other

29:37.440 --> 29:45.120
circuits and I think it was it was Ollie Bearman who also skipped FREC right? Like I think he also

29:45.680 --> 29:51.360
jumped from– but, but he went from Italian and German F4 he did both of those. Italian F4 and

29:51.360 --> 29:59.360
German F4. I think both even two seasons so he knew the circuits they were like he knew definitely

29:59.360 --> 30:11.280
Monza, Imola. I'm pretty sure that they've also did some rounds potentially in Austria or Belgium.

30:11.280 --> 30:17.200
I'm not quite sure–and I don't want anyone to quote me on this–but also having the knowledge

30:17.200 --> 30:25.120
of Italian circuits, the circuits like Austria is closer than British circuits are to Austria.

30:25.680 --> 30:30.880
Yeah anyways, I think we should, um look at the feature race, where it was a really fun battle

30:30.880 --> 30:38.480
for the win towards the end between Freddie, Ugo and Stromsted. I think Freddie kind of

30:38.480 --> 30:44.720
cooked his tires towards the end but I think that's maybe partly true because they all are

30:44.720 --> 30:49.920
the top ones in the podium, they all have a little bit of F3 experience, but he still had some quite

30:49.920 --> 30:54.880
good defensive moves against– I think, he can just take a lot to learn, I mean, he was in the lead

30:55.520 --> 31:01.440
I mean, I'm sad he didn't win. I think he could have won, he had the pace to do so, I think,

31:02.000 --> 31:05.840
I think, just keeping the tires is so important this weekend, and I think he just

31:06.480 --> 31:12.800
slightly let go of them towards the end. I think it's the same conversation as we have with F2

31:12.800 --> 31:20.320
where we all kind of expected Freddie and Trident to just run away with the title, especially after

31:20.320 --> 31:26.400
the performance shown in recent years, but it's pretty clear to me that–I'm going to be honest

31:26.400 --> 31:32.880
and I'm going to say– I think Campos is overall the stronger car and stronger team. It's coming from

31:32.880 --> 31:41.040
you and not from me, meaning if I say that, I'm obviously biased. Because you know, I feel like

31:42.160 --> 31:48.240
I mean, in the team's championship, Trident isn't even second, they’re third, they're still behind

31:48.240 --> 31:54.320
Van Amersfoort. Yeah because I mean de Palo's just been not doing anything, I think he's

31:54.320 --> 32:03.360
also really struggling. Yeah, but also I expected also more from the duo of you know, Stromsted and

32:05.440 --> 32:13.040
Slater like we expected quite a lot from. Apart from this weekend, Stromsted has actually not

32:13.040 --> 32:19.120
been doing–if I take away this weekend's performance–he's not actually been performing that well- I mean

32:19.120 --> 32:25.040
of course, he's won this weekend. Yeah that's why I'm trying to point out, going into 2025 we all

32:25.040 --> 32:31.520
expected Stromsted to be–honestly, I didn't expect him to win like the championship, but I expected

32:31.520 --> 32:37.360
him to be someone you would see on the podium quite a handful of times you know? He's one of

32:37.360 --> 32:41.440
those drivers, I need to check before I say something wrong and I get cancelled again.

32:42.240 --> 32:52.240
Like Formula Regional, he was with RPM and he still scored a pole and handful of podiums

32:53.360 --> 33:03.280
and you know it's not that, he's like the greatest driver ever, but especially with a team like Trident

33:03.280 --> 33:09.600
I expected him to be up there I expected him to do something

33:10.720 --> 33:16.880
you know, but this far he has scored a handful of points, he's like p6 in the championship, I think he

33:16.880 --> 33:27.200
was also up there last season but it's not what we've been expecting and it's definitely not

33:28.160 --> 33:37.600
what should happen when you're in a team that scored, you know the last three championships

33:38.480 --> 33:44.400
for a reason. Yeah but I mean, at least the championship, I mean Freddie still, I still

33:44.400 --> 33:50.160
hope he can do something, you know one DNF for Ugo, Freddie gets some points but still not

33:50.160 --> 33:55.520
too far apart. I mean, I'm still gonna believe in Freddie, of course I am, but I mean, he's, I mean he is 

33:55.520 --> 34:02.720
only 16 points behind Ugo, yeah it's not a massive gap. Simultaneously, Theophile Nael–who I

34:02.720 --> 34:08.240
kind of have a soft spot for– is also only 10 points behind Freddie and we saw he had a bad

34:08.240 --> 34:14.320
weekend but the season’s far from over, everything can still happen, a bad crash can still happen at

34:14.320 --> 34:23.200
the wrong time and suddenly I think, I'll say that I think these three should probably make up the

34:23.280 --> 34:29.440
championship. Bruno Del Pino is also only three points behind so I could also see him kind of

34:29.440 --> 34:38.000
get in the mix, but he's not a driver that we've been seeing so much from but he's been very

34:38.000 --> 34:45.120
consistent recently. Definitely, even looking at the teams Campos have nearly a 50 point lead in the

34:45.120 --> 34:50.880
team's championship which is insane. Before we move on to the team's championship actually I

34:50.880 --> 34:58.560
wanted to mention that this feature race was the first time this season that Del Pino didn't score

34:58.560 --> 35:05.680
any points I'm pretty sure. Yeah I just wanted to point that out, because he is maybe not the one

35:05.680 --> 35:12.480
who's like always winning and he won one race and had one further podium but he had a streak of

35:12.480 --> 35:19.520
seven consecutive points finishes and that stuff that later can put you into the championship

35:20.160 --> 35:28.640
uh position especially if like –I think we saw it at some point– where Ugochukwu crashed, I think it was

35:28.640 --> 35:36.000
Spain, um where it was like please correct me if I'm wrong, but there was kind of like overtaking

35:36.000 --> 35:42.000
stuff going on he was running pretty well and then crashed and it's like those things can cost you

35:42.000 --> 35:47.920
very very valuable points and if you're someone who's consistently fighting for the win that's

35:47.920 --> 35:54.320
more likely to happen than if you're someone who's in the points very comfortably, but –and

35:54.320 --> 36:01.840
also obviously fighting but not quite up there for like really p1 aggressively–sometimes it's

36:01.840 --> 36:08.960
good idea to take points home with you and you gain more because the guys in front of you

36:09.600 --> 36:15.280
aren't happy enough and crash each other out. Yeah, he's been doing a really solid job, I think.

36:16.080 --> 36:21.280
VAR, while they're not maybe as fast as Campos or Trident, they're still doing quite

36:21.280 --> 36:26.480
solid, they are second in the team's championship but they– and they have been doing quite a solid

36:26.480 --> 36:31.440
job I think with all three of their drivers they, they're still they're always getting solid

36:31.440 --> 36:37.040
points they're always somewhere there in the points pretty much. Yeah, of course, VAR are only

36:37.040 --> 36:43.680
six points ahead of VAR on the team's championship, but I think it's going to be Campos who wins this

36:43.680 --> 36:48.560
I mean it's quite– You mean six points ahead of Trident? You said VAR is six points ahead of VAR.

36:49.920 --> 36:56.800
No, other way, VAR is six points ahead of Trident within the standings but I think that's mainly

36:56.800 --> 37:01.920
due to de Palo just not getting points for Trident. I think, Trident are maybe slightly-

37:01.920 --> 37:09.680
It varies week to week it's like last time out in Spain, Trident were awful and VAR are really good

37:10.400 --> 37:16.560
so maybe that this wins between them for who be second fastest I think we'll have a good fight

37:16.560 --> 37:22.640
for second in the team's championship, I think first is probably- unless something like really dramatic

37:22.640 --> 37:28.640
happens– I think first is pretty locked in. They get disqualified or- That happened to a team in GB4

37:28.640 --> 37:34.160
actually one of the GB4 teams got disqualified from the entire championship! There’s precedent for it.

37:34.160 --> 37:38.560
But yeah, unless something like majorly dramatic happens I think our fight that we should be

37:38.560 --> 37:44.800
looking out for is for P2 in the team standings. Does anyone have anything to say about F3?

37:44.800 --> 37:53.600
No, it was kind of boring. So the E4 season starts this weekend and it's been a very long time

37:53.600 --> 37:58.160
since we've done a season preview because everything has already started like ages ago

37:58.160 --> 38:07.200
but E4 is… it's not really a new championship, it's just a bit of a side championship to Italian F4

38:07.200 --> 38:18.560
after ADAC F4 folded. A D A C. Okay whatever, okay ADAC F4 folded– We have to bring the German stuff.

38:19.680 --> 38:28.800
Okay um, and it started in 2023 so it's not really it's kind of new so we've had only three

38:28.800 --> 38:36.960
champions obviously, uh Ugo Ugochukwu won the first one, uh Akshay Bohra won in 2024 and KNB won in 2025

38:36.960 --> 38:44.400
Akshay, come back we miss you. KNB was the first driver to win both Italian F4 and E4 in

38:44.400 --> 38:50.720
the same year so we'll see if another driver can do the same thing or not, but like I said E4 is

38:50.720 --> 38:59.040
very new so. It's incredibly similar to Italian F4, in terms of the grid. A few minor differences

38:59.040 --> 39:09.120
but it's basically Italian F4 2.0. Yeah, so Prema lineup, they have seven cars again– Um wait, I have

39:09.120 --> 39:14.480
a question, I have a question: do we get points for the top 15 or top 10? I don't know if they've

39:14.480 --> 39:19.440
changed it for E4 or not actually. They changed it for Italian but I don't know if they changed it

39:19.440 --> 39:27.440
for E4. I hope they also do the 15 points, because otherwise. I mean the grid is only 36 cars this time

39:27.440 --> 39:33.680
I imagine they would because they're both run by ACI, aren't they? Yeah but yeah so

39:34.240 --> 39:41.200
Prema lineup is the same seven cars as Italian F4 except Cosma has been replaced by

39:41.200 --> 39:46.880
Georgiy Zasov? I don't know if I said that right but I'm a little bit surprised that Cosma's not

39:46.880 --> 39:52.560
there just kind of because Aksoy had a bad weekend last time but he's the highest

39:52.560 --> 39:59.040
Prema in the standings so to not see him there. It reminds me a bit of um R-ace last year where

39:59.040 --> 40:07.840
Olivieri was doing Italian F4, but just didn't show up for Euro 4 or E4. E4, it was Euro 4 and then it

40:07.840 --> 40:12.160
got changed to E4 last year. But I think it makes kind of sense but yeah. Because you have to keep

40:12.160 --> 40:18.560
in mind the f***ing money that running all these championships cost, and I can imagine that if

40:18.560 --> 40:26.160
you want to focus on Italian F4, you probably are getting you know out- outbidded if that’s

40:26.160 --> 40:32.400
even a word, by other folks who you know want to drive but maybe only turned 15 recently or

40:33.280 --> 40:39.840
don't feel like doing a whole season or you know, I can imagine and I can understand why someone

40:39.840 --> 40:45.440
wouldn't do both. I think it's not doing both, I think it's purely maybe down to funding honestly,

40:45.440 --> 40:51.200
that's as it always is in racing. Yeah but like imagine you ask your team but you're also doing

40:51.200 --> 40:57.520
Italian F4 and you're like “hey, how about E4” and they're like “no, this guy has a lot more money and

40:57.520 --> 41:04.400
he just does not usually care for Italian F4” and I feel like Italian- I feel like E4 is also one of

41:04.400 --> 41:11.280
those things where we see more guys from other championships, you know, try out a bit of experience

41:11.280 --> 41:18.960
on the Italian circuits. We'll get to that in a second because there is a couple drivers.

41:20.640 --> 41:27.440
So, US Racing has the exact same lineup as they do for Italian F4 so Sammalisto, Savinkov, Killion,

41:27.440 --> 41:39.760
Bansal, Busso, and- who am I missing? Um, Kräling, I'm missing a driver. Bansal? I said Bansal.

41:40.400 --> 41:47.840
She said Bansal, Kräling. Just say Bansal twice. Speaking of Bansal, um. I'm go- okay I'm going

41:47.840 --> 41:52.960
to issue an apology, I'm going to because I made a mistake, and I'm very very sorry for this and I

41:52.960 --> 41:58.640
actually, they don't know but I actually have to do two apologies because one is really old but I still

41:58.640 --> 42:05.040
feel bad about it and I have nightmares about it. The first apology is I said that Ary Bansal, uh, I said

42:05.040 --> 42:10.400
something along the lines of uh, “he's kind of rookie because he only did GB4” which was wrong

42:10.400 --> 42:17.600
you see, he also did British F4 and some other stuff, I think he did E4 last season no? I think so yeah.

42:18.320 --> 42:24.240
The thing is, the embarrassing part of that is actually that I saw him race live in British F4 so I

42:24.240 --> 42:31.280
don't know how the f**k I forgot that I saw him, and I'm pretty sure I even saw him score- win, I'm

42:31.360 --> 42:39.200
fairly sure about that. I don't know how the f**k I missed that, but I am pretty sure that I must

42:39.200 --> 42:45.280
have mixed him up with someone else. And when I think of Ary Bansal, I do not think of British

42:45.280 --> 42:50.400
F4, I think of GB4, because that was the season he won the championship which was really cool

42:50.400 --> 42:54.480
and a second one, and I'm really still embarrassed about this because you'll need to shoot me for

42:54.560 --> 43:02.480
this, is that very very early in the season, when we were talking about F1 Academy, I said that Tina

43:02.480 --> 43:09.280
Hausmann did not do other things, but that's also wrong because she did do, uh, I think it was Italian

43:09.280 --> 43:16.000
F4 and E4 at some point, and she didn't even do it too badly. And I’m really embarrassed about it because

43:16.640 --> 43:22.640
I- I need to shoot myself because, I always say that I have everything in mind about drivers

43:22.640 --> 43:26.240
but for some reason I forgot that and I must have also mixed it up with someone else.

43:27.200 --> 43:33.360
And I'm so embarrassed I'm going to retire. Um I just remembered which driver I forgot in the

43:33.360 --> 43:38.560
US Racing lineup, I forgot Robinson. We are going to get murdered by the Spanish fans.

43:40.160 --> 43:46.880
But yeah so, R-ace GP, their lineup from Italian F4, we have Andy, Kenzo and Tamás, but um.

43:47.840 --> 43:54.480
We- I don't know where Emily is, she's not here, and so, the fourth and fifth cars are

43:54.480 --> 44:02.720
going to the Karras brothers from Spanish F4. I cannot lie to you, I don't know how they're

44:02.720 --> 44:11.280
doing in Spanish F4. E4 kind of feels like a filler episode in an anime. I've- from the announcements

44:11.360 --> 44:16.960
that R-ace made, Payton Westcott should be doing one round of E4, but I don't know how

44:16.960 --> 44:22.000
we're going to be doing that. Do we have a sixth car for her, is she going to take the place of one

44:22.000 --> 44:28.240
of the other drivers? I mean we saw it in other seasons where you know, drivers would miss one

44:28.240 --> 44:36.000
race and I don't know- has anyone checked if Spanish F4 overlaps with E4? I have not checked

44:36.000 --> 44:41.920
actually. Because I can imagine that being a thing. As far as I know Emily's not doing

44:41.920 --> 44:48.880
anything in E4 this year, like she's not competing in it at all, which is kind of sad. And I wonder if

44:48.880 --> 44:58.320
it's money or- or in which direction it is maybe money, because either it's she cannot afford it

44:58.320 --> 45:04.720
and she's saying hey maybe- or she can afford it, but maybe she's saying she wants to take the money

45:04.720 --> 45:13.760
for else things like testing or doing other stuff. Or, what I could also imagine the Karras brothers

45:13.760 --> 45:18.880
because I also don't know them, maybe they've got a lot of money and if you only have a limited

45:18.880 --> 45:25.840
number of cars and they say “hey, we're paying more than she does.” I think they do have a lot of money,

45:25.840 --> 45:34.320
I just don't know how much a lot of money is. I mean if you have two children and they’re brothers

45:34.320 --> 45:40.000
and, you know you're the parent and you can fund both their racing careers, you gotta have a bit of money.

45:40.720 --> 45:46.560
Yeah. Uh, to answer the Spanish F4 questions, the Karras brothers are not doing very well.

45:47.200 --> 45:55.200
Um, they've only scored points twice each, and yeah there is a round that, um, coincides from

45:55.200 --> 46:01.680
SF4 and it's the Monza round. Maybe they just really like the Italian circuits. So, would they

46:02.480 --> 46:08.160
I- would they um prioritize E4 or Spanish F4 do you think? Cause like if they're not doing very

46:08.160 --> 46:12.480
well in Spanish F4, and they want to gain experience elsewhere, I could see them

46:13.120 --> 46:19.760
prioritizing going to Monza instead, but who knows. I could see that but I also would think

46:19.760 --> 46:26.960
Spanish F4 is the longer running championship that they're doing but we'll see. I mean if you want to

46:26.960 --> 46:33.600
track who sponsors what are you going to say, “hey maybe I didn't have the best season, but

46:33.600 --> 46:37.840
you know I raced all-” I think it's seven or eight rounds or something like that.

46:38.720 --> 46:45.760
you know, “I improved” or do you say “hey, in this championship where I have nine races in general”

46:46.720 --> 46:52.560
I think if you want to use E4 to advertise yourself, you have to do really good.

46:53.440 --> 47:00.000
And if I was a driver I would not risk that. What team do they race for in Spanish F4? I have absolutely

47:00.000 --> 47:10.320
no idea. G4. Uh, so not a very good team. R-ace is quite a good team, so I could see them doing

47:10.320 --> 47:15.840
at least better than they are in Spanish F4. But also, you have to imagine the possibility if you go in

47:16.560 --> 47:21.600
you do really well, and then instead of returning for a second weekend, you just leave and you say

47:21.600 --> 47:27.760
“hey I could have done even more, but I didn't want to” even if you couldn't have done better.

47:28.800 --> 47:35.760
Just like a bit of stat padding, you know. Um, so that's the first cameo from drivers in other

47:35.760 --> 47:41.680
championships. We also have George Proudford-Nalder in again with AKM. I hope he can have a better time

47:41.680 --> 47:50.960
out. Yeah um, for him and also luckily for Andy Consani, is that because the grid is 36 cars,

47:51.680 --> 47:56.880
there is no fourth race so there is no possibility for either of them to

47:56.880 --> 48:03.600
not qualify for the third- for their third race. You say that now just for them to DNS.

48:04.560 --> 48:11.120
Oh. Whatever happens, it's better than a DNQ, okay. You're saying this now but

48:11.760 --> 48:20.240
are we sure that they can't DNQ. I mean- yeah there’s only three races. Yeah but what I'm saying

48:20.240 --> 48:30.160
is, if they don't show that they can qualify and just all their laps are below the, I mean I assume

48:30.160 --> 48:36.800
in Italian F4 there's another rule. I think if this happens, if that somehow happens, I think Robin

48:36.800 --> 48:47.680
will actually come for you. Just gonna show up at your house. I don't bring bad luck, I just bring good luck. Um, so anyways, a lot of

